<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Maní</h1><p class="st-meta">1441 CE – 1542 CE (approx.) · capital Maní</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Maya province of the Tutul Xiu lineage in western Yucatán, which allied with the Spanish in 1542 and preserved some of the richest Maya records.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutul-xiu">Xiu</a>, who claimed origins outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a> and links with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uxmal">Uxmal</a>, led the revolt that destroyed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/league-of-mayapan">League of Mayapan</a> around 1441. They then founded a province centred on Maní, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puuc">Puuc</a> hills, which became one of the most powerful in the peninsula.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Maní was ruled by a halach uinic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutul-xiu">Xiu</a> lineage, the Tutul Xiu, who governed many towns through local batabs. Its feud with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cocom">Cocom</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sotuta">Sotuta</a> flared in 1536, when the Xiu lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ah-dzun-xiu">Ah Dzun Xiu</a> was murdered at Otzmal. Xiu scribes kept genealogies and later compiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-chilam-balam-of-mani">Book of Chilam Balam of Maní</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1542 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ah-kukum-xiu">Ah Kukum Xiu</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tho">T'ho</a> and submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-younger">Montejo the Younger</a>, giving the Spanish a vital ally. In 1562 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Diego de Landa</a> held his notorious auto-da-fé at Maní, burning Maya books. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutul-xiu">Xiu</a> family retained noble status in colonial times.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1441 CE – 1542 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry